#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120580} :activity/id #uuid "2660cd04-6f2d-5dd7-95c2-55cf96cdbf95" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049196 :topic/id #uuid "e289a183-5534-5388-8ee7-3fc0d8f4ee68" :topic/math-academy-id 1833} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121002 :tutorial/content "The **curvature** of a curve measures how much the curve \"bends\" at a given point. It can also be thought of as a measure of how much the curve deviates from being a straight line. Curvature is denoted by the Greek letter $\\kappa$ (pronounced \"kappa\"). \n\nIf $\\kappa=0$ at some point on a curve, then that curve looks like a straight line in some neighborhood of the point.\n\nIf the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ is parametrized by the arc length $s,$ then the curvature $\\kappa$ is the magnitude of the change of direction of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T$ per unit of arc length. That is,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) = \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|= \\| \\mathbf T'(s) \\|.\n$$\n\nNote that $|| \\mathbf v ||$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(s)= \\left\\langle \\sin\\left(\\dfrac s2\\right), \\: \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}s}{2}, \\: -\\cos\\left(\\dfrac s2\\right)\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \nwhich is parametrized by arc length $s.$ Let's find the curvature of  $\\mathbf r(s)$  at the point where $s={\\pi}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(s) =\\dfrac 12 \\left\\langle \\cos\\left( \\dfrac s2\\right), \\: \\sqrt{3}, \\: \\sin \\left(\\dfrac s2\\right)\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate $\\mathbf{T}(s)$ and evaluate its absolute value at $s=\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{s}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{s}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{s}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{'}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature by taking the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{'}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** To calculate $\\kappa,$ the function $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ should be at least twice differentiable with respect to $s.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d74577d5-5fe6-5544-bd86-b20fee42defa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2402 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "12125b22-92e2-567c-9dc7-87e721cb0430" :step/math-academy-id 10650 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119564 :question/id #uuid "219df52e-8833-58df-8a2f-ee53cfed5640" :question/math-academy-id "e-3135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9befd973-a2d9-567a-8808-d126ecaaae1b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351528}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization And the Unit Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "9b2d3984-cd37-5bc8-b3ce-f427ae5bd607" :step/math-academy-id 10651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119565 :question/id #uuid "ea963bd4-6c5b-5385-8efc-dcd4de104da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3140"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c9d719f-4936-5994-90eb-c33da57639cc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375923}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given a General Parametrization"} :step/id #uuid "cd83eef1-8999-5702-814d-b4f011d666e1" :step/math-academy-id 10657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119566 :question/id #uuid "2db0218f-bfb3-552a-8683-2f32f230a3ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-3141"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c819c5c-aa18-5887-ace3-dfbd9c5700e1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377777}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature Given the Arc Length Parametrization"} :step/id #uuid "3a00b3b6-447c-5ccc-97a8-7cd65929b702" :step/math-academy-id 10658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121007 :tutorial/content "If the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is, remember that the arc length $s$ and the parameter $t$ are related by the formula \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle s(t)= \\int_0^t\\left\\| \\mathbf  r'(x)\\right\\|\\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nBy the chain rule and the fundamental theorem of calculus, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d s} \\dfrac{\\textrm d  s}{\\textrm d t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm d  \\mathbf T}{\\textrm d s} \\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}‖ & =‖\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}s}‖‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =κ(t)‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ \\\\ κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{'}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3b41506-4cd1-5589-b3a6-4cd3f4ecbdb2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2484 :tutorial/title "The Curvature of Curves Parametrized Using a General Parameter"} :step/id #uuid "d9dd47a2-479b-5929-a71d-4ad46370bfb7" :step/math-academy-id 11099 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120582}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Curvature" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120033}]}
